                  <handDesc>
                     <handNote xml:id="h1" script="Ethiopic">
                        <seg type="script">Eighteenth-century script (?).</seg>
                        <seg type="ink">Black, red, deep vermilion, strong vermilion.</seg>
                        <desc>Very fine and careful, regular.</desc>
                        <seg type="rubrication">Divine names; names of the donors; incipits and
                           headings of the texts (a few lines are rubricated, alternating with black
                           lines on <locus target="#2 #57rb #78vb #109rb #144r #162r #180r #198rb #214vb #223vb"/>;
                           elements of punctuation marks; elements of Ethiopic numbers; names of the days of the week in both texts.</seg>
                     </handNote>
                  </handDesc>
                  
                  <additions>
                     <list>
                        <item xml:id="a1"> 
                           <locus target="#233rb"/>
                           <desc type="AcquisitionNote">The note states that the owner of the manuscript is <persName role="owner" ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS15366TaklaHaym">Takla Hāymānot</persName>. 
                              It is written in the same hand that added the name of Takla Hāymānot in the supplication formulas throughout the manuscript.</desc>
                           <quote xml:lang="gez">ዝመጽሐፍ፡ ዘተክለ፡ ሃይማኖት፡ ዘአጥረዮ፡ በንዋዩ፡ ዘሠረቆ፡ ወዘፈሐቆ፡ ዘእንበለ፡ ፈቃደ፡ አበምኔት፡ በሥልጣነ፡ ጴጥሮስ፡
                              ወጳውሎስ፡ ውጉዝ፡ ለይኩን። ። ።</quote>
                           <quote xml:lang="en">This is the book of Takla Hāymānot who acquired it with his own money. The one who
                              stole (what is written in) it or the one who scratched it without the permission of the abbot shall be excommunicated by the authority of
                              Peter and Paul.</quote>
                        </item>
                        
                        <item xml:id="a2">
                           <locus from="233va" to="234va"/>
                           <desc type="GuestText">Excerpt from <title type="complete" ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LIT1146Argano">ʾArgānona wǝddāse</title>; it is written in a different, less regular and skilled
                              hand. The list of the saints mentioned is somewhat different from the standard version. At the end of the text, the name of <persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS15381AsrataMaryam">ʾAsrāta Māryām</persName> is mentioned in a supplication.</desc>
                        </item>
                        
                        <item xml:id="e1">
                           <desc>The title of <ref target="#ms_i2"/> is crudely written on the back board with yellow paint.</desc>
                        </item>
                        
                        <item xml:id="e2">
                           <locus target="#1r"/>
                           <desc>Writing exercises.</desc>
                        </item>
                        
                        <item xml:id="e3">
                           <locus target="#161vb"/>
                           <desc>The last two lines of <ref target="#ms_i2.2"/> are written in a different hand.</desc>
                        </item>
                        
                        <item xml:id="e4">
                           <locus target="#9rb #17vb #23ra #49rb #73ra"/>
                           <desc> Some lines of <ref target="#ms_i1"/> are washed out and replaced with a text written in a
                              different hand (possibly the same hand as the one that wrote the name of the <persName role="owner" ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS15366TaklaHaym">Takla Hāymānot</persName>). The folia listed above are a few examples.</desc>
                        </item>
                        
                        <item xml:id="e5">
                        <locus target="#132r"/>
                           <desc>Shelfmark C4-IV-55</desc>
                        </item>
                        
                        <item xml:id="e6">
                           <desc type="findingAid">Names of the days of the week indicating daily
                              readings are written in the centre of the upper margin, in <ref target="#ms_i1"/>
                              (<locus target="#2r #16r #39v #57r #78v #96r #109r"/>) and in <ref target="#ms_i2"/> (<locus target="#124r #144r #162r #180r #198r #214v #215r #223v #224r"/>;
                            they are rubricated, written between two dotted lines.</desc>
                        </item>
